-### Cross-View Consistent Loss
-Cross-View Consistent Loss penalizes discrepancies between the residuals of paired views, weighted by their structural similarity, to enforce global coherence and mitigate Gaussian overfitting [S6, unofficial]. It selects adjacent view pairs from the set of $M$ views, retaining only pairs with an SSIM value greater than 0.6 [S6, unofficial]. The loss is computed as $LCVC = \text{SSIM}(I^g_t, I^g_j) \times \text{L1}(I^g_t - I_i, I^g_j - I_j)$, where $I^g_t$ and $I^g_j$ are ground truth images for views $i$ and $j$, $I_i$ and $I_j$ are rendered images, and L1 is the L1 norm between residuals [S6, unofficial]. The SSIM threshold for view pair selection is 0.6 [S6, unofficial].
+### LBFC (Bottleneck Feature Consistency Loss)
+LBFC (Bottleneck Feature Consistency Loss) enhances feature-level consistency between original input rays and flipped reflection rays [S6]. It is a regularization loss that enforces consistency between bottleneck features of original and flipped reflection rays using Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD) [S6]. It computes the JSD between the probability distributions of the bottleneck features of the original and flipped rays [S6]. It extracts bottleneck feature $b$ from the network encoder [S6]. LBFC with JSD on $b$ achieves PSNR 19.55, SSIM 0.767, LPIPS 0.180, and Average Error 0.101, outperforming MSE (PSNR 18.83) and cosine similarity (PSNR 18.77) [S6]. Regularizing $b$ with MSE or cosine similarity yields no significant performance impact [S6]. Applying LBFC with JSD to $b_{\hat{d}}$ (bottleneck feature conditioned on viewing direction $\hat{d}$) degrades performance, likely due to reduced feature dimensionality limiting representation capacity [S6].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of rays/pixels and $S$ is the number of samples per ray. The gradient computation adds a similar factor depending on the differentiation strategy (AD, Adjoint, or Analytic) [44-radiance-field-optimization.md].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the differentiation strategy. Reverse-mode AD requires $O(N \cdot S)$ memory to store intermediate states [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Adjoint methods and analytic inversion reduce this to $O(N)$ or constant memory [44-radiance-field-optimization.md].
-*   **Strengths:** Directly enforces fidelity to the input data, leading to high reconstruction accuracy when the input data is clean and complete [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Prone to overfitting noise in the input data. Can lead to artifacts in regions with missing data or occlusions if not combined with regularization [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When the primary goal is accurate reconstruction of known data and the input data is reliable.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the input data is very noisy, sparse, or when the goal is to generate novel views without ground truth (e.g., in generative settings).
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels/samples in the projection and the number of parameters in the scene representation, as it requires a full forward pass and backpropagation through the projector [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the differentiable implementation of the projector. Standard AD requires storing intermediate states for backpropagation, leading to high memory usage [44-radiance-field-optimization.md].
+*   **Strengths:** Directly minimizes the reconstruction error, ensuring the output matches the input data [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Can be sensitive to noise and outliers due to the L2 norm [S1, unofficial]. May lead to overfitting if used alone without regularization [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When the primary goal is accurate reconstruction of measured data, such as in CT reconstruction or novel view synthesis with dense inputs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When the input data is very noisy or sparse, or when structural/semantic consistency is more important than pixel-wise fidelity [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Data Consistency Loss originates from the field of computed tomography (CT) and inverse problems, where it is used to minimize the difference between measured projections and forward-projected estimates [S1, unofficial]. In the context of differentiable volume rendering and neural radiance fields, it has been adapted as the primary reconstruction loss, leveraging the differentiability of the volume rendering equation [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. It is widely used in NeRF, Neuralangelo, and other neural implicit surface reconstruction methods [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial].
+Data Consistency Loss originates from the field of inverse problems and computed tomography, where it is used to enforce that the reconstructed image is consistent with the measured projections [S1, unofficial]. In the context of differentiable volume rendering and neural radiance fields, it has been adapted to minimize the difference between rendered images and ground truth images, serving as the fundamental supervision signal for training [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in various neural rendering frameworks, including NeRF and its variants [S1, unofficial].
 
